 "headline": "Marked to fuel each day, the estimate of United's third-quarter adjusted EPS moved from $3.00 a share on 15 July 2026 to $2.54 by 6 October 2026, against a guided $2.50 to $3.50; United's shares moved -14.1% against the market over the same days.",
 "plain_summary": [
  "Marked to fuel each day, the estimate of United's third-quarter adjusted EPS moved from $3.00 a share on 15 July 2026 to $2.54 by 6 October 2026, against a guided $2.50 to $3.50; United's shares moved -14.1% against the market over the same days.",
  "The expected price paid for the quarter's fuel went from $3.69 a gallon on the guidance day to $3.93; United assumed about $3.69. Its lowest reading was $3.62 on 4 August 2026 and its highest $3.95 on 10 September 2026.",
  "On the last day the estimate is $2.54 a share (P10 $2.18, P90 $2.82); with no recovery it would be $2.42, and with the share United stated, $2.91.",
  "The quarter closed on 30 September 2026. From 30 September 2026 the estimate is settled: every fuel price of the quarter is known, so it does not move again until United reports.",
  "This is the forward call's method on earlier dates, not a second forecast, and no trading rule is tested here.",
  "From today's fuel price ($4.67 a gallon paid, against $4.19 in the second quarter), the simulator puts 2026Q4 operating income at $753 million (P10 -$806 million, P90 $1,934 million) with measured behaviour."
 ],
 "limits": [
  "Each day's estimate uses the cost clock refitted through the second-quarter 2026 report, which came out with the guidance on 15 July after the market closed; the first row therefore uses a report published that evening.",
  "The range is the same on every day: the spread of United's earlier misses against its own midpoint. It does not narrow as the quarter's fuel prices come in.",
  "The stock series is United's log return minus beta times the S&P 500 fund's, summed from the day after guidance, with beta from the previous 252 sessions. It is shown beside the estimate, not explained by it.",
  "Days on which no fuel price was published show no spot price; the estimate then uses prices up to the day before.",
  "The forward block is the simulator's view under stated assumptions (today's fuel held for twelve months, demand at plan), not a forecast of reported results.",
  "No delayed share price reaction to fuel was found by the market clock, so the gap between the two lines is not a trading signal."
 ],
